The Wronskian of three functions y_1(x), y_2(x), y_3(x) is defined as:W(y_1, y_2, y_3) = \begin{vmatrix}
  y_1 & y_2 & y_3 \\
  y_1' & y_2' & y_3' \\
  y_1'' & y_2'' & y_3''
  \end{vmatrix}

We will show that W(y_1, y_2, y_3) = 0 for all x, which implies that y_1, y_2, y_3 are linearly dependent, contradicting our assumption.

Since each y_i(x) is a solution to the differential equation, they satisfy:y_i'' + P(x) y_i' + Q(x) y_i = 0 \quad \text{for} \quad i = 1, 2, 3

Rewriting the second derivatives:y_i'' = -P(x) y_i' - Q(x) y_i

Substitute y_i'' into the Wronskian:W(y_1, y_2, y_3) = \begin{vmatrix}
  y_1 & y_2 & y_3 \\
  y_1' & y_2' & y_3' \\
  - P y_1' - Q y_1 & - P y_2' - Q y_2 & - P y_3' - Q y_3
  \end{vmatrix}

Factor out -1 from the third row:W(y_1, y_2, y_3) = -\begin{vmatrix}
  y_1 & y_2 & y_3 \\
  y_1' & y_2' & y_3' \\
  P y_1' + Q y_1 & P y_2' + Q y_2 & P y_3' + Q y_3
  \end{vmatrix}

Now, notice that the third row is a linear combination of the first and second rows:P \times \text{(Second Row)} + Q \times \text{(First Row)} = \text{Third Row}

This means that the three rows are linearly dependent, and therefore, the determinant of the matrix (the Wronskian) is zero:W(y_1, y_2, y_3) = 0

Since the Wronskian W(y_1, y_2, y_3) = 0, it contradicts our initial assumption that y_3(x) is linearly independent of y_1(x) and y_2(x). Therefore, any third solution must be a linear combination of y_1(x) and y_2(x).

